# Legacy Vite remains local-only. The Next web app (apps/web) proxies its
# same-origin /api/v1 calls to Express; it never exposes an API URL to browsers.
# API_PROXY_TARGET is server-only and is resolved into Next rewrites when a
# production build is created; the dev server reads it when it starts. If unset,
# the safe localhost fallback is http://localhost:3001, so production deployment
# configuration should always supply its Express origin explicitly.
# API_PROXY_TARGET=http://localhost:3001
#
# Express API (apps/api): copy these into your uncommitted local environment.
# Production values must be supplied by the deployment secret manager. API_ORIGINS
# is an exact comma-separated allowlist; wildcards are intentionally rejected.
# NODE_ENV=development
# PORT=3001
# API_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:5173
# LOG_LEVEL=info
# TRUST_PROXY=false
# RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000
# RATE_LIMIT_MAX=100
# Session lifetime is bounded to 60 seconds through 31 days; cookies and database
# expiry use this same value. Authentication endpoints have a separate limiter.
# SESSION_TTL_SECONDS=604800
# AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=900000
# AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=10
#
# Local PostgreSQL for the persistence workspace (compose.yaml):
#
#   DATABASE_URL=postgresql://poker_range_trainer:poker_range_trainer_local@localhost:54329/poker_range_trainer
#
# This is deliberately a development-only credential and port. Do not reuse it
# for a deployed database; provide the production connection string through the
# deployment secret manager instead.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://poker_range_trainer:poker_range_trainer_local@localhost:54329/poker_range_trainer
#
# The mobile app has ONE optional variable, set in `mobile/.env.local` (or as
# an EAS build env var), never committed:
#
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=<your Sentry DSN>
#
# It turns on crash reporting (mobile/platform/crashReporting.ts). Unset, the
# app runs with reporting fully inert: no init, no network, no console noise.
#
# Three further variables are BUILD-time only. They are never read by app code,
# so they do not belong in mobile/.env.local; they are set as EAS secrets:
#
#   SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN, SENTRY_ORG, SENTRY_PROJECT
#
# All three are needed for source maps to upload. The Sentry plugin entry in
# mobile/app.json carries no organization/project, so the generated
# ios/sentry.properties falls back to SENTRY_ORG and SENTRY_PROJECT from the
# environment. With either unset, sentry-cli has no destination it can resolve
# and fails with a configuration error; the iOS bundle phase turns that into a
# build failure rather than a silent unsymbolicated build, since neither
# SENTRY_DISABLE_AUTO_UPLOAD nor SENTRY_ALLOW_FAILURE is set in this repo.
# See LAUNCH-CHECKLIST.md step 7.
